{"title":"Local heroes: London’s XO Bikes – breaking the chain","link":"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/local-heroes-xo-bikes","date":1791472573000,"content":"<p><a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/\">Cyclist</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/local-heroes-xo-bikes\">Local heroes: London’s XO Bikes – breaking the chain</a></p>\n\n<p>A stone’s throw from the south bank of the Thames, along the Blackfriars Road, lies an Edwardian building that looks a lot like an old pub. The ‘Sons of Temperance Friendly Society’ lettering is smartly maintained on the building’s frontage, only now there is a large neon XO sign visible in the window. That’s XO for ex-offender.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We give blokes with a past – and bikes with a past – the chance of a decent future,’ says XO founder Stef Jones. ‘We operate two – soon to be three – prison workshops, and a training programme in Lewisham for ex-offenders.’</p>\n\n\n\n<p>XO Bikes is a charitable enterprise that runs training both inside and outside prisons. As Jones puts it, ‘We’re a purpose with a business, not a business with a purpose.’ Every pound of profit is reinvested into wages, running costs or workshop materials.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>When talking to the graduates of the XO Bike Academy now working at the Blackfriars workshop, one word arises more than any other to describe what the course has given them: structure.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘Our students learn a trade, build up experience and potentially get a job, and we will encourage them to move onwards in the industry,’ says Jones. ‘We try to give them the belief that they can succeed at something and have a sense of belonging in society. That’s often something these guys won’t have had.’</p>\n\n\n\n<h2>A new path</h2>\n\n\n\n<p>XO Bikes has a strong relationship with the justice system and the police. And what have the police got? Lots of stolen bikes.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We would argue that by giving us a bike that keeps a student busy for a week, pays his wages and helps to keeps him out of prison, a single bike could be worth tens of thousands of pounds to society, the government and the taxpayer,’ says Jones.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>On the day <em>Cyclist </em>visits, we talk to guys at different stages of their mechanical journey (for now they are all men; XO has run workshops in women’s prisons and there has been one female graduate, but Stef says he wants to improve the course’s gender diversity). The feedback borders on the evangelical. Many aren’t cyclists and don’t plan to be – one trainee tells us he tried to take on a 30-minute ride and it took him almost two hours, and he hasn’t been back on a bike since – but it’s the work itself that is sustaining.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>‘We’re not a bike business, we’re a person business,’ says Jones. ‘The product is someone who doesn’t go back to prison – they just happen to fix bikes.’</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Find out more at <a href=\"https://xobikes.com/\" target=\"_blank\">xobikes.com</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p><a href=\"https://www.cyclist.co.uk/in-depth/local-heroes-xo-bikes#h-xo-bikes-stories\"><img src=\"/media/628d4b1017f9c307e63bdb9fc8b90c98d5e6cbed1b5213a3d06e5c1e28a5971f.webp\" alt=\"\" /><em><strong><mark>Article continues below…</mark></strong></em></a></p>\n\n\n\n<figure><img loading=\"lazy\" width=\"647\" height=\"970\" src=\"/media/382e433619056a9abbf75d6b0d049d878f654e7ff382380f684f630ad5a3fcc6.webp\" alt=\"Local heroes XO Bikes\" srcset=\"/media/382e433619056a9abbf75d6b0d049d878f654e7ff382380f684f630ad5a3fcc6.webp 647w, /media/be4d8e26767d72a0aeb241f0a3cf51dce5b89187513a459e32e8acd512e71656.webp 200w, /media/84d8b73f39d841c0f72a72be35bcabdd979b476ac23d1549e20c3f04f693358a.webp 768w, /media/e018377ca778b3f495507461f5746e842b9dd2f9a9148f03cc95e530e2694b0c.webp 1024w, /media/eefccddfd84584253b8767814c54170e44076f82b5747533990d542a8d5005bb.webp 1365w, /media/005ae41dccfc10b07042fa13c9ece7c963fe9e8359cae30da108d76def071e6a.webp 750w, /media/019fed8f6b843d35c298cd8f58f4a2cab7ab831468842db06fa37ec3761d